<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" target="israel_gaza_lebanon::military_kinetic_intensi" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A rise in hostile strikes against commercial and naval vessels in the Strait is often part of a coordinated escalation by Iran-aligned actors that prompts reciprocal strikes and higher-tempo military operations across adjacent theaters, increasing the aggregate kinetic tempo in Israel–Gaza–Lebanon.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">israel_gaza_lebanon</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" target="israel_gaza_lebanon::great_power_engagement" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained attacks on international shipping in the Strait trigger naval deployments, sanctions threats, and diplomatic initiatives from major powers to secure trade routes, drawing those powers into deeper posture and mediation efforts related to regional crises including Israel–Gaza–Lebanon.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">israel_gaza_lebanon</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" target="north_korea_threat::military_escalation" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in hostile attacks in the Strait forces U.S. and coalition forces to conduct kinetic reprisals and redistribute naval assets, creating windows of perceived U.S. distraction and lowering adversary risk thresholds that prompt increased kinetic tests or strikes on the Korean Peninsula.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">north_korea_threat</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" target="russia_ukraine_war::interceptor_stocks" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A sustained rise in hostile incidents in the Strait of Hormuz prompts Western and regional partners to divert interceptor missiles and air‑defense expendables to Gulf bases and carrier groups, reducing inventories available for transfer or prepositioning to the Russia–Ukraine theater.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" target="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher maritime attack frequency forces reassignment of ISR, targeting assets, and munitions stockpiles to protect shipping and regional facilities, degrading surveillance and strike support in Ukraine and enabling adversary increases in drone strike tempo.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::critical_component_avail" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Kinetic attacks, seizures, and strikes on commercial vessels rapidly destroy, delay, or divert shipments of semiconductors and other critical parts transiting the strait, directly reducing immediate imports and inventories available to tech producers.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_uncertainty" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in hostile incidents triggers emergency policy reviews and public debate in Washington and Beijing over supply‑chain safeguards and export/sanctions responses, raising firms' uncertainty about imminent regulatory actions affecting tech trade.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
