<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::transit_control_dominanc" target="north_korea_threat::regional_cohesion" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A concentration of transit control (e.g., unilateral command or sustained overseas tasking) requires prolonged redeployment of U.S. and partner military assets and signals unilateral crisis management, which undermines trust and coordinated burden‑sharing among East Asian security partners.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">north_korea_threat</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::transit_control_dominanc" target="russia_ukraine_war::counterdrone_coverage" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A move toward concentrated transit control (e.g., tighter state enforcement in Hormuz) elicits focused defensive deployments and prioritization of limited counter‑drone systems to the Gulf, reducing the proportion of critical nodes covered in the Russia‑Ukraine zone.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::transit_control_dominanc" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_controls_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.55</data>
  <data key="d37">If authority over transit is centralized in an adversarial state or bloc, policymakers in the U.S. and allied states are more likely to broaden or tighten export controls to prevent state interception or diversion of dual‑use technologies.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
