<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" target="north_korea_threat::military_escalation" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained high drone attack rates in Ukraine accelerate the spread of low‑cost attack‑drone designs, components and operational tactics via observable doctrine and illicit supply chains, enabling North Korea to scale similar strike capabilities and thus raise the intensity of kinetic engagements on the peninsula.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">north_korea_threat</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" target="strait_hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">An increased tempo of kinetic and improvised drone strikes in the Russia–Ukraine war drives wider availability, tactics, and operational learning that are exported to regional actors and proxy groups who then employ drones to strike or harass commercial vessels and offshore infrastructure in the Strait, raising the measured maritime attack frequency.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" target="strait_hormuz_standoff::transit_control_dominanc" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in drone threats against shipping and coastal infrastructure prompts littoral states and major naval powers to centralize enforcement and movement controls (convoys, military escorts, no‑sail zones), increasing concentration of transit decision‑making authority.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_attack_rate" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_controls_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">A sharp rise in documented use of commercially available drones and related components in Ukraine prompts US and allied policymakers to rapidly expand and tighten export controls on drones, optics, and dual‑use components that could be diverted to military use — including items that are commonly exported to China.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
