<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_controls_level" target="israel_gaza_lebanon::great_power_engagement" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Tighter export controls on advanced tech provoke reciprocal diplomatic/military signaling between Washington and Beijing (public statements, envoy activity, naval/air posture) that spills into Middle East diplomacy, prompting both great powers to increase direct engagement around the Israel–Gaza–Lebanon crisis to defend influence and narratives.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">israel_gaza_lebanon</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_controls_level" target="north_korea_threat::regional_cohesion" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Tightened export controls force allies and regional partners to choose between economic access to China and alignment with US security policy, generating policy disagreements and commercial frictions that erode trust and coordinated action among neighbours.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">north_korea_threat</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_controls_level" target="strait_hormuz_standoff::insurance_premiums" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Tighter export controls escalate perceived bilateral tech confrontation and supply‑chain risk, prompting insurers to reprice political/war risk for transits through the Strait and immediately raise marine insurance premiums.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
