<edge source="north_korea_threat::military_escalation" target="israel_gaza_lebanon::great_power_engagement" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Intense North Korean military tests or strikes force the US and allied commanders to reallocate naval, air and missile‑defense assets and senior diplomatic bandwidth to East Asia, reducing the depth and tempo of US-led diplomatic and military engagement in the Israel–Gaza–Lebanon theater.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">north_korea_threat</data>
  <data key="d40">israel_gaza_lebanon</data>
</edge>
<edge source="north_korea_threat::military_escalation" target="russia_ukraine_war::interceptor_stocks" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A surge in North Korean kinetic activity prompts the US and regional partners to hold back or reallocate interceptor missiles and air‑defence shipments to reinforce Indo‑Pacific inventories, reducing exportable interceptor stocks for Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">north_korea_threat</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="north_korea_threat::military_escalation" target="strait_hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">High-intensity North Korean military escalation drives U.S. and allied carrier/strike-group redeployments to Northeast Asia, temporarily reducing naval deterrence and surveillance in the Persian Gulf and thereby enabling adversaries and proxy actors to increase kinetic harassment of commercial shipping in the Hormuz corridor.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">north_korea_threat</data>
  <data key="d40">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="north_korea_threat::military_escalation" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_uncertainty" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Acute increases in military escalation prompt governments to signal or prepare emergency export controls, sanctions, and contingency regulation, which immediately raises firms' uncertainty about future technology policy and enforcement.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">north_korea_threat</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
